{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '930,980p;1360,1426p;1505,1524p'\"","aggregated_output":"   930\t   against its live state and, if discharged, starts the install child\n   931\t   (section 7.3). On a matching refusal, or a failed discharge, the\n   932\t   program resumes with the refusal raised as the effect's error, the\n   933\t   same way an `:ffi` `error` raises. On `gap` the honest outcome is a\n   934\t   `gap`, and the composition's policy decides.\n   935\t\n   936\tRevision r1 claimed steps 3 to 7 needed no new engine machinery. That\n   937\twas wrong in two places: the wait entry needs the two-state shape above,\n   938\tand installation needs the child task below. Park entries, kept cursors,\n   939\t`handle-stream-block`, and `check-wait-set` are reused; `:link-request`,\n   940\t`:link-response`, and the install child are new.\n   941\t\n   942\t### 7.3 Installing a module is a scheduler child task\n   943\t\n   944\tThe linker delivers a verified image with its obligations. Installing it\n   945\tis the scheduler's, as an explicit child evaluation with its own state:\n   946\t\n   947\t```mermaid\n   948\tstateDiagram-v2\n   949\t  [*] --> loading: 5b discharged\n   950\t  loading --> running: kernel loader accepted the image\n   951\t  loading --> refused: loader defect\n   952\t  running --> parked: child effect blocks (stream, ffi, require)\n   953\t  parked --> running: child wait satisfied\n   954\t  running --> validated: halt; every export bound in child store\n   955\t  running --> refused: halt; :export-missing, or error\n   956\t  validated --> linked: bindings published to registry value\n   957\t  linked --> [*]\n   958\t  refused --> [*]\n   959\t```\n   960\t\n   961\tRules:\n   962\t\n   963\t- **The child is a task, not a call.** The scheduler holds\n   964\t  `:installs {name {:phase p :vm child :waiters [task-ids] :parent\n   965\t  link-id}}`. The child is a VM of the same backend with a fresh,\n   966\t  isolated store containing nothing of any other task's state (UCF\n   967\t  section 7.6.2 applied to loading), its own wait-set, and the parent's\n   968\t  registry value as its module view. The scheduler steps it in its\n   969\t  ordinary round beside every other task; nothing about it runs inside\n   970\t  wait restoration.\n   971\t- **The requiring task waits on the install, not on the linker.** Once\n   972\t  the child starts, the parent's entry becomes\n   973\t  `{:reason :install :name 'foo}`, and any other task that requires\n   974\t  `'foo` meanwhile joins `:waiters`. No task is restored until the\n   975\t  install reaches `linked` or `refused`.\n   976\t- **`running` may park.** A module body that reads a stream, calls FFI,\n   977\t  or requires another module parks the child exactly as any task parks;\n   978\t  a transitive require emits its own link request under its own id\n   979\t  (section 7.4). The parent is parked on `:install` and is unaffected;\n   980\t  the scheduler keeps stepping every other task. There is nothing to\n  1360\t\n  1361\tThe namespace keeps its shape and changes its contents.\n  1362\t\n  1363\t| Today                        | After                                       |\n  1364\t|------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|\n  1365\t| `register-module r name fns` | `register-host-module r name fns profiles`  |\n  1366\t|                              | (rules below); no code fetched, because the |\n  1367\t|                              | host is the boundary                        |\n  1368\t| `resolve-module r sym`       | unchanged signature; reads `:bindings`      |\n  1369\t| `require-handler`            | registry hit answers; installing joins the  |\n  1370\t|                              | waiter set; miss mints, parks, and emits    |\n  1371\t|                              | (section 7.2); never throws for absence     |\n  1372\t| `register-effect-handler`    | unchanged                                   |\n  1373\t| `stream-module`              | a host module; `register-stream-module`     |\n  1374\t|                              | calls `register-host-module`                |\n  1375\t| `default-registry`           | unchanged                                   |\n  1376\t| (new) `link-module r m d s`  | the `linked` transition of section 7.3      |\n  1377\t\n  1378\t**`register-host-module` enforces UCF profile classes.** Every binding\n  1379\tmust carry a profile in the shape of `yin.vm/primitive-profiles`, and\n  1380\tregistration refuses (throws, as a host assembly defect detected before\n  1381\tany operation runs) unless the profile's class is `:pure`, or\n  1382\t`:effectful` with a declared, non-empty `:yin.k/effects` set, and\n  1383\t`:yin.k/host-state` is `:none`. A `:host`-class function, a function\n  1384\twith undeclared host state, or a binding with no profile is refused. An\n  1385\t`:effectful` export must return plain effect data for the engine to\n  1386\tinterpret; it may not perform IO. `stream/make` is exactly such a pure\n  1387\teffect constructor, and the engine's `:make-stream` capability remains\n  1388\tthe explicit boundary that performs the creation. A host module is\n  1389\tentered as an already-linked manifest with `:yin.module/tree` absent,\n  1390\t`:yin.module/derivations {}`, and its exports listed under\n  1391\t`:yin.module/primitives` by profile address.\n  1392\t\n  1393\t**The host module is the trusted composition boundary, and the check\n  1394\tabove is a declaration check, not a proof.** A host function is opaque\n  1395\thost code: nothing the linker or the engine can inspect proves that a\n  1396\tfunction declared `:pure` performs no IO or holds no state. The profile\n  1397\tis a warranty the composition gives, in the same sense UCF section 7.5.2\n  1398\tcalls a `:host-state :none` claim \"a publication's warranty\". The\n  1399\tobligations therefore fall where they can be met:\n  1400\t\n  1401\t- *The composition* is responsible for every host module it registers:\n  1402\t  the function behaves as its profile declares, is reviewed and tested\n  1403\t  as trusted code, and is published with its profile record so a\n  1404\t  receiver can check equality. Registering a host module extends the\n  1405\t  trusted computing base of every VM that composition wires, and a\n  1406\t  composition should register the fewest it can.\n  1407\t- *The linker* verifies what is verifiable: profile shape and class at\n  1408\t  registration, profile-address equality at discharge (step 5b), and\n  1409\t  that no host function ever arrives by linking. It makes no claim\n  1410\t  beyond those.\n  1411\t- *Runtime confinement*, where a composition needs it, is not a linker\n  1412\t  mechanism. It is the capability discipline of\n  1413\t  `docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`: an effectful host function\n  1414\t  receives the resources it may touch as explicit capability tokens in\n  1415\t  its effect's interpretation, and a token it was not handed it cannot\n  1416\t  use. That discipline belongs to the engine's effect handlers and the\n  1417\t  composition's wiring, and it is the only way a `:pure` or\n  1418\t  `:effectful` declaration becomes enforceable at run time. Until a\n  1419\t  composition wires it, a host module's declaration is trusted, and this\n  1420\t  document says so rather than implying a sandbox it does not provide.\n  1421\t\n  1422\tThe registry value's `:modules` map changes from `{name {sym fn}}` to\n  1423\t`{name {:manifest m :address a :derivation d :bindings {sym value}}}`.\n  1424\tNo shim keeps the old entry shape: this is a dev-only repository with no\n  1425\tdeployed registries, and a clean break is the default here.\n  1426\t\n  1505\tstore is `:shadowed-free`; a free name the manifest never declares is\n  1506\t`:undeclared-free`; two outstanding requires on one response stream\n  1507\teach restore on their own id; a response landing before the entry would\n  1508\thave been polled is not skipped; a module requiring another links\n  1509\ttransitively while a third task keeps running; a require cycle is\n  1510\t`:require-cycle`; `(vm :stack)` and `(vm :register)` in `yin.repl` link\n  1511\tthe same manifest by H and R respectively and produce B0-equal results.\n  1512\t\n  1513\t**M5. `yin.repl` wiring.** The REPL composes the link pair per VM and\n  1514\tthe content pair per connection (`connect` already opens the RPC\n  1515\tclient). `(require 'foo)` at the prompt exercises the whole path.\n  1516\t\n  1517\tCoexistence during M4: host modules are entered by\n  1518\t`register-host-module` from the first commit of M4, so every existing\n  1519\tcomposition keeps working while linked modules are added. There is no\n  1520\tperiod in which two registry shapes coexist.\n  1521\t\n  1522\t## 10. File box\n  1523\t\n  1524\t```text\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
