{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '395,430p;547,570p;640,680p'\"","aggregated_output":"   395\t :yin.k/stack    [ … ]                     ; St per the table above, encoded\n   396\t :yin.k/env      { sym → encoded value }   ; E\n   397\t :yin.k/k        [ {:yin.k/frame-type :return                    ; or :eval-call / :request-sent,\n   398\t                    :yin.k/segment :segment/sha256-…             ; which also carry :yin.k/call-id\n   399\t                    :yin.k/pc 5 :yin.k/env {…} :yin.k/stack-base 0} … ]   ; K, innermost last\n   400\t :yin.k/pending  {…}}                      ; the wait it was in, §7.4.3 — one variant per reason\n   401\t```\n   402\t\n   403\t**Quiescence.** The migration unit is one task: the whole blocked machine\n   404\t(§7.6.3). Lifting additionally requires the machine's ready-queue to be\n   405\tempty — a machine with runnable work queued is mid-scheduling, its next\n   406\tconfiguration is not the parked one, and lift answers `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n   407\t\n   408\t### 7.4.2 Safepoint maps for foreign engines: static facts only\n   409\t\n   410\tThe reference machine needs no metadata because its registers *are* the\n   411\tcanonical configuration. A foreign engine — a register allocator over the\n   412\timage, a WebAssembly lowering, a JIT — keeps its state in physical locations\n   413\tof its own choosing, and the first draft promised it reconstruction data\n   414\tthat is not a function of the code: absolute stack depth and environment\n   415\tkey sets at a pc depend on the *activation* — a closure enters with the\n   416\tcaller's residual operand stack and an environment formed from its captured\n   417\tenvironment plus arguments (`apply-call`), and §2.6 enforces no equal stack\n   418\theights at joins, so the same pc is legitimately reached at many depths.\n   419\tNone of that is derivable, and none of it needs to be: **the frame carries\n   420\tit.** E, St, and every K frame's `:stack-base` and env travel in the value;\n   421\ta foreign engine lowers the canonical frame directly.\n   422\t\n   423\tWhat *is* a function of the canonical vector alone, and what such an engine\n   424\tmust publish — **per segment and per engine profile**, derived from the\n   425\tcanonical stream at its own load time — is the static half:\n   426\t\n   427\t```clojure\n   428\t[[sp :yin.safepoint/segment      :segment/sha256-3f1a…]  ; the code, by address\n   429\t [sp :yin.safepoint/engine       :wasm32/v1]             ; the engine profile that produced this layout\n   430\t [sp :yin.safepoint/pc           5]                      ; canonical resume pc\n   547\tthe path.\n   548\t\n   549\tThe first draft encoded markers as ordinary maps — `{:yin.k/primitive '+}`,\n   550\t`{:yin.k/ref addr}` — while passing program map literals through in their\n   551\tnatural shape. That is ambiguous to everyone: §2.5 admits map literals,\n   552\tso a program can *contain* `{:yin.k/primitive '+}`, and a decoder cannot tell\n   553\tan encoder's marker from a program's data. The grammar here is disjoint by\n   554\tconstruction: **every map at an encoded-value position is a UCF marker**, and\n   555\ta program's own maps are always wrapped.\n   556\t\n   557\t| Encoded form | Tag | Contents | Decodes to |\n   558\t|---|---|---|---|\n   559\t| scalars: nil, boolean, number, string, keyword, symbol | — | themselves (never a map; cannot forge a marker) | themselves |\n   560\t| vector, list, set | — | same shape, elements recursively encoded (never a map) | same shape, elements decoded |\n   561\t| literal map | `:yin.k/literal` | `:yin.k/entries [k₁ v₁ k₂ v₂ …]`, keys and values recursively encoded | the map, keys and values decoded |\n   562\t| closure | `:yin.k/closure` | `:yin.k/segment addr :yin.k/entry pc :yin.k/params […] :yin.k/env {encoded}` | `{:type :closure …}` (§2.4) |\n   563\t| reified continuation | `:yin.k/frame` | a nested frame (§7.4.1), recursively | `{:type :reified-continuation …}` |\n   564\t| parked continuation | `:yin.k/frame` | a nested frame plus `:yin.k/parked-id` | `{:type :parked-continuation …}` |\n   565\t| primitive | `:yin.k/primitive` | `:yin.k/name sym` | the named function, after profile check (§7.5.2) |\n   566\t| stream reference | `:yin.k/stream` | `:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …` | an attached handle, under a fresh store key |\n   567\t| cursor reference | `:yin.k/cursor-ref` | `:yin.k/cell cell-id` | a store cursor entry for that cell, §7.5.3 |\n   568\t| shared subvalue | `:yin.k/ref` | `:yin.k/id addr` | the table entry at `addr`, §7.5.3 |\n   569\t| anything else | — | — | **refused**: `:yin.k/non-portable` |\n   570\t\n   640\t  presence.** For each required name, the resumer must hold a primitive\n   641\t  whose profile address is equal; a same-named function of different profile\n   642\t  is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the symbol and the expected profile — it is\n   643\t  a routing decision for whoever chose this resumer, not a silent\n   644\t  substitution.\n   645\t- The standard map `yin.vm/primitives` must be published with profiles\n   646\t  (note `yin/def` is `:effectful` with effect `:vm/store-put`, and `require`\n   647\t  is `:effectful` with `:module/require`); that publication is an acceptance\n   648\t  blocker (§7.11).\n   649\t\n   650\t### 7.5.3 Sharing, cells, and cycles\n   651\t\n   652\t**The value table.** A configuration is a DAG, not a tree: a closure's\n   653\tcaptured environment is the frame's environment; a value on the stack is\n   654\talso bound in `E`. Encoding naively duplicates, and a deep K makes\n   655\tduplication quadratic. The encoder therefore builds a **value table**:\n   656\t\n   657\t- On encountering a collection, closure, or continuation it has already\n   658\t  encoded (host identity during one lift), it emits\n   659\t  `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/ref :yin.k/id addr}` where `addr` is the `dao.jing`\n   660\t  address of the encoded subvalue, and places the subvalue in\n   661\t  `:yin.k/values {addr → encoded}`.\n   662\t- A subvalue is placed in the table when it is referenced more than once or\n   663\t  its encoded size exceeds a composition-supplied threshold; otherwise it\n   664\t  stays inline. Both choices yield equal semantics; only the table is needed\n   665\t  for correctness of sharing.\n   666\t- A cycle (a value reachable from itself through host identity) is refused\n   667\t  as `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :cyclic` naming the path. The\n   668\t  reference machine cannot produce one — `E` is extended by `merge`, never\n   669\t  mutated — so the refusal guards against foreign engines and bridge-returned\n   670\t  host structures. Cycles *through ref data* cannot be minted at all and are\n   671\t  rejected at decode (§7.5.1); this single representation retires the first\n   672\t  draft's two competing ones.\n   673\t\n   674\tBecause table entries are content-addressed, a carrier that already holds an\n   675\tentry (a `dao.jing` intake pool, a DHT replica) need not carry it again, and\n   676\ttwo continuations parked from the same closure share its encoding. This is\n   677\t`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.3's checkpoint-as-published-fold applied to\n   678\ta single frame. It is also why §7.3.4 separates `:yin.k/id` (which varies\n   679\twith these choices, harmlessly) from `:yin.k/occurrence` (which does not).\n   680\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
