{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\\\\(defn free-names|\\\\(defn ast-requirements|store-put-keys' src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/code_as_tuples.cljc docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | head -n 70; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '255,285p;385,410p;520,532p;880,894p'\"","aggregated_output":"rg: src/cljc/yin/vm/code_as_tuples.cljc: No such file or directory (os error 2)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1353:(defn free-names\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1504:(defn ast-requirements\n   255\t :row-defect-fn       (fn [value] defect-or-nil)    ; step 2, per part\n   256\t :validate-fn         (fn [value] defect-or-nil)    ; step 4, whole\n   257\t :obligations-fn      (fn [value] [sym ...])        ; step 5a: free names\n   258\t :definitions-fn      (fn [value] #{sym ...})       ; step 5a: own defs\n   259\t :parts-fn            (fn [value] [address ...])}   ; step 2 worklist\n   260\t```\n   261\t\n   262\t- `:identity-fn` mints an identity from a payload. It is used by\n   263\t  `publish!` and never by verification.\n   264\t- `:identity-matches-fn` is the step-3 check, directed by the identity.\n   265\t  For H and R it is `(= identity (image-hash value))`, a contract-pinned\n   266\t  hash with one algorithm. For the storage-derived formats it is\n   267\t  `(jing/segment-matches? identity value)`, so a value minted under\n   268\t  `:sha256` while the default is `:blake3` verifies under the algorithm\n   269\t  the identity names and never fails spuriously.\n   270\t- `:row-defect-fn` validates one fetched part on its own, before any\n   271\t  address it references is enqueued: tag, arity, slot kinds, saturation.\n   272\t  For single-payload formats it is the whole validator, run early.\n   273\t- `:parts-fn` returns the storage addresses a fetched payload references\n   274\t  and without which it is incomplete. For the three single-payload\n   275\t  formats it returns nothing. For `:yin.ast/code` it returns the child\n   276\t  row ids of a row body.\n   277\t- `:obligations-fn` returns the free-name occurrences of a value with\n   278\t  their positions. `:definitions-fn` returns the constant store keys the\n   279\t  value itself binds at module level, with their positions. The linker\n   280\t  discharges occurrences by defined-before-use and joins the remainder\n   281\t  with the manifest's declarations to form obligations (step 5a). For\n   282\t  the AST record both are Datalog over the rows (`yin.vm/free-names`\n   283\t  extended with the occurrence path, and the store-key query behind\n   284\t  `yin.vm/ast-requirements`); for the vector formats both are operand\n   285\t  scans by pc (`:var` against `:store-put`, `:load-free` against the\n   385\t     yields the **definition set** with positions: every constant store\n   386\t     key the image binds at module level (the section 7.7 store-key\n   387\t     query of `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`: `:vm/store-put` rows,\n   388\t     `:store-put` operands, `yin/def` applications with a constant key),\n   389\t     each with the position of the binding in the main sequence.\n   390\t     A definition discharges an occurrence by **defined-before-use**,\n   391\t     not by subtraction of names:\n   392\t     - an occurrence in the main sequence (outside every lambda body)\n   393\t       is discharged only by a definition of that name that precedes it\n   394\t       in main-sequence order (for a tree, in the top-level `:do` body\n   395\t       order; for a vector, by pc). A main-sequence occurrence whose\n   396\t       only definitions follow it is `:use-before-definition`: in the\n   397\t       isolated store it would read nothing, and no manifest\n   398\t       declaration may stand in for a name the module itself defines;\n   399\t     - an occurrence inside a lambda body is discharged by a definition\n   400\t       of that name anywhere in the main sequence. The body runs only\n   401\t       when the closure is applied; an application in the main sequence\n   402\t       is itself a main-sequence occurrence the first rule orders, and\n   403\t       an application after export happens after halt, when every\n   404\t       definition has run. A body that is applied in the main sequence\n   405\t       before a name it reads is defined is not caught here; it fails\n   406\t       in the child at run time as an ordinary error, and section 7.3's\n   407\t       `refused` phase reports it.\n   408\t     Each undischarged occurrence is joined with the manifest that\n   409\t     delivered the image (section 8.1): a name under\n   410\t     `:yin.module/primitives` becomes\n   520\t{:format              :yin.ast/code\n   521\t :contract            \"v2\"\n   522\t :identity-fn         (fn [body] (jing/segment-key body))\n   523\t :identity-matches-fn (fn [root-id body] (jing/segment-matches? root-id\n   524\t                                                                  body))\n   525\t :row-defect-fn       (fn [body] (row-local-defect body))\n   526\t :validate-fn         (fn [tree] (yin.vm/validate-rows tree))\n   527\t :obligations-fn      (fn [tree] (yin.vm/free-names (relation tree)\n   528\t                                                    (occurrences tree)\n   529\t                                                    (:root tree)))\n   530\t :definitions-fn      (fn [tree] (store-put-keys (relation tree)))\n   531\t :parts-fn            (fn [body] (body-child-ids body))}\n   532\t```\n   880\t   (the root task at start, each child at `loading`), unique among every\n   881\t   task that has ever written to that pair; `counter` is the task's own\n   882\t   engine gensym. The scheduler never reuses an origin tag and a task\n   883\t   never reuses a counter, so no two requests on one pair share an id.\n   884\t   A composition that multiplexes several schedulers onto one pair must\n   885\t   give each scheduler a distinct origin prefix as composition data;\n   886\t   two schedulers on one pair with one prefix is a host assembly defect.\n   887\t4. **The request is appended; `ok` moves the entry to the response\n   888\t   state.** The handler appends `:envelope` through the link request\n   889\t   writer the composition supplied in `opts`. On `ok` the entry becomes\n   890\t   `{:reason :link-response :link-id 7 :response ... :cursor ...}`. On\n   891\t   `full` the entry stays in `:link-request` with its envelope retained\n   892\t   verbatim and is retried on the next poll, the `:ffi-request`\n   893\t   discipline. The two states are the two UCF pending variants this\n   894\t   flow adds (section 11, item 10): `:link-request` carries everything\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
